# The Apothecary Diaries — World Lore

## Description

The Apothecary Diaries is set in a vast fictional eastern empire whose court, bureaucracy, medicine, architecture, etiquette, and social order draw heavily from historical East Asia without matching one real dynasty. The setting is grounded rather than conventionally magical. Its dangers come from politics, disease, poison, secrecy, ignorance, corruption, and violations of social expectations.

The emperor rules through nobles, civil officials, military officers, provincial administrators, merchants, artisans, farmers, servants, and laborers. Rank shapes where a person may go, whom they may address, what information they can access, and how much protection society affords them. The imperial capital is the political and cultural center of the realm, dominated by the enormous Imperial Palace.

## Government and Court

The empire is a hereditary monarchy supported by a large bureaucracy. Officials manage taxation, law, records, diplomacy, agriculture, infrastructure, ceremonies, provinces, and military affairs. Court factions form around noble families, officials, military houses, consorts, eunuchs, regional interests, and personal loyalties. Conflict is often indirect. Marriage, rumor, reassignment, bribery, forged evidence, appointments, blackmail, poisoning, and succession maneuvering can accomplish more than open violence.

## The Imperial Palace

The palace functions like a city within a city. Walls and gates divide courtyards, pavilions, gardens, offices, kitchens, archives, storehouses, guard posts, servant quarters, clinics, workshops, and residences. Access is controlled. Servants, guards, eunuchs, officials, physicians, craftsmen, couriers, and ladies-in-waiting each have places they are expected to remain; entering the wrong compound can create suspicion.

The Outer Court is the administrative side of imperial rule, where ministries, military offices, clerks, messengers, guards, and officials conduct state business. The Rear Palace houses the emperor's consorts and their attendants alongside eunuchs and approved personnel. It is both a household and a dynastic institution intended to protect the imperial bloodline and provide a controlled environment for producing and raising heirs.

Despite its luxury, the Rear Palace is restrictive and competitive. High-ranking consorts occupy their own pavilions; lower-ranking consorts live more modestly and may rarely receive imperial attention. Rank influences residence, clothing, food, allowance, servants, and authority. Imperial favor matters alongside health, fertility, family background, beauty, education, usefulness, and reputation.

## Medicine, Poison, and Health

Medicine is practical, empirical, and uneven. Apothecaries, physicians, midwives, herbalists, servants, merchants, and families may possess useful knowledge, but training varies enormously. Remedies include herbs, roots, teas, powders, ointments, poultices, minerals, animal products, alcohol, oils, incense, and dietary treatments. Diagnosis relies on symptoms, pulse, history, smell, environment, and experience rather than modern laboratory testing.

Some treatments work while others are ineffective or dangerous. Dosage matters: the same substance may be medicine in one quantity and poison in another. Cosmetics, incense, cookware, pigments, plants, metals, food, smoke, and contaminated water can cause illness without anyone recognizing the source. Court physicians possess formal authority, but hierarchy can discourage contradiction or admission of uncertainty. Practical specialists may notice causes that prestigious physicians overlook.

Poison is a particular concern in elite households because food and medicine pass through many hands. Important people may use food tasters, trusted attendants, sealed containers, controlled kitchens, and restricted suppliers. Yet apparent poisoning may instead result from spoilage, toxic plants, mold, metals, cosmetics, or mistaken medicine. Deliberate poisoning is attractive because symptoms can resemble natural illness. Access often matters more than rare ingredients.

Infection, parasites, contaminated food, unsafe water, and epidemics remain serious threats. Childbirth is especially dangerous. Pregnancy, miscarriage, bleeding, infection, malnutrition, and complications threaten mothers and children, while an imperial heir's survival can affect national politics. Wet nurses, physicians, attendants, food, bathing, cosmetics, and nursery conditions can therefore become politically sensitive.

## Society, Law, and Family

Society is strongly hierarchical. Nobles, officials, merchants, artisans, laborers, servants, entertainers, criminals, and bonded workers do not experience law or opportunity equally. Family reputation is extremely important; scandal can damage relatives, marriage prospects, employment, and alliances. Adoption, concubinage, illegitimacy, and uncertain parentage can become sensitive.

Punishments can include beating, dismissal, confiscation, imprisonment, forced labor, exile, or execution. Confessions, witnesses, seals, records, physical evidence, and official reputation matter, but status can distort investigations. A servant accusing a noble may require extraordinary proof or powerful protection.

Marriage is often a family arrangement and, among elites, a political instrument. Noble marriages connect households. Concubinage is accepted among powerful men, while women face stricter expectations concerning sexuality and reputation. Women can nevertheless wield informal influence through household management, commerce, patronage, medicine, family networks, or proximity to powerful people.

## The Pleasure District

The capital's pleasure district is an established part of urban life, containing brothels, teahouses, entertainment houses, restaurants, merchants, musicians, courtesans, servants, apothecaries, and residents. High-ranking courtesans are trained entertainers whose value may depend on conversation, music, poetry, games, etiquette, beauty, and reputation as much as sexual availability.

The district has its own hierarchy, debts, contracts, rivalries, and protections. Workers possess varying degrees of freedom; some may leave through earnings or patronage while others remain trapped by debt. Because nobles, officials, soldiers, and merchants visit, the district is also an information network where gossip can eventually influence palace affairs.

## Economy, Knowledge, and Daily Life

Coinage and market trade coexist with household production, taxation, stipends, gifts, and barter. Cities support specialized merchants selling medicine, spices, cloth, jewelry, paper, books, cosmetics, food, and tools. Agriculture supports the state, so failed harvests can produce debt, migration, banditry, disease, and unrest.

Literacy is valuable and unevenly distributed. Officials, scholars, merchants, physicians, clerks, and elite households are more likely to read than ordinary laborers. Seals, ledgers, medical texts, notices, contracts, family records, and correspondence carry authority and may become evidence or targets for forgery. A low-ranking person able to read records, identify an herb, recognize a crest, or understand court protocol may possess dangerous knowledge.

## Crime, Investigation, and Intrigue

Information is often more useful than force. Servants overhear conversations, physicians know private illnesses, courtesans hear officials speak freely, eunuchs control access, clerks control records, and merchants know who buys unusual goods. Blackmail, hidden parentage, forged correspondence, poison, sabotage, bribery, theft, smuggling, concealed illness, and manipulation of servants are credible threats.

Investigations depend on opportunity, access, motive, physical evidence, medical facts, testimony, relationships, and contradictions. Solving a mystery does not guarantee justice. A correct accusation made by the wrong person can be dangerous, while someone of high status may remain protected unless evidence is undeniable or a stronger patron intervenes.

## Tone and GM Principles

Run the setting as grounded historical mystery and social drama. There are no conventional supernatural powers. Apparent curses should normally have natural, medical, social, or criminal explanations unless canon establishes otherwise.

The palace should feel beautiful, orderly, and dangerous simultaneously. Gardens, silk, incense, music, fine food, and ceremony coexist with disease, coercion, jealousy, political calculation, and fragile health. Status matters constantly, but low-ranking people still possess agency through expertise, information, discretion, alliances, and access.

Everyday activities should create opportunities for play: cooking, medicine, gossip, markets, laundry, correspondence, etiquette, household logistics, festivals, and travel. Canon establishes the world and its institutions rather than a mandatory sequence of events. Once play diverges, follow consequences naturally while preserving hierarchy, political incentives, medical limitations, and the grounded tone.

## Knowledge Boundaries

Established facts include the imperial monarchy, Outer and Rear Palace, consort hierarchy, eunuchs and palace servants, practical medicine, social inequality, family reputation, and the political importance of illness and poisoning.

Common beliefs include curses, omens, supernatural causes of disease, and assumptions based on rank or gender. Expert interpretation comes from physicians, apothecaries, scholars, officials, experienced servants, merchants, and specialists, but experts can be wrong.

Rumor is information, not truth. Palace gossip, accusations of infidelity, secret parentage, poisoning, and conspiracy may be accurate, distorted, or deliberately planted. Private imperial affairs, secret illnesses, hidden relationships, and conspiracies should remain concealed until discovered through play.